From 3bdf35f5690011d405d9a000cd21ed42230d1ab3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Siyuan Feng <sealseacat@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 23:31:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] feat(masking): masked values stay valid for their data
 type
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

format_random treated everything as free text, so masked output could be
*invalid* for its own type: dates became impossible calendar entries
(8342-73-51), UUID hex positions picked up letters g-z, IPv4 octets went to
999, card numbers failed Luhn, and typed Python values came back as strings
that stricter engines reject on write (and that make masked data trivially
distinguishable from real data).

New strategies, wired as the defaults for their detection rules:
- fake_uuid        a real, deterministic version-4 UUID (case/braces kept)
- fake_ip          valid IPv4 octets (1-254) / IPv6 hex with grouping kept
- fake_credit_card random digits, separators kept, Luhn-valid
- fake_date        deterministic +/-30-730 day shift; always a real date in
                   the input's own representation (date object or string)

format_random/shuffle now dispatch typed values (bool, int, float, Decimal,
date, datetime, UUID) to type-preserving logic — an int stays an int with
the same digit count, a float still parses, a bool passes through.

fake_email now lands on the RFC-reserved, undeliverable example.invalid
domain by default; the previous behavior (identifying domains survive) is
available explicitly as fake_email_keep_domain.

Seed-map reuse coerces stored text back to the original's Python type so
recorded pairs also write cleanly into typed columns.

